  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe will use a basis-function expansion of the step function to systematically explore the optimization space. The central idea is to decompose the step function into components (basis functions) that ease the optimization of the maximum correlation. The method reduces the effective number of variables by leveraging symmetries and reducing dimensionality. We parameterize the step function using a combination of rectangular pulses arranged in a quasi-periodic lattice, which allows us to satisfy both the unit integral constraint and the [0,1] bounds through an explicit construction.\n\nThe rationale is that this parameterization:\n- Provides meaningful control over the function shape while respecting constraints\n- Enables more effective gradient-based optimization by working in a reduced-dimension space\n- Explores a structured subspace that may contain better solutions than arbitrary search\n- Can be systematically refined starting from a parent solution\n\nLimitations: The approach relies on parameterization and may not discover truly novel solutions, but it allows for controlled exploration and principled dimensionality reduction.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nVariables:\n- Number of basis functions: M (to be determined)\n- Parameters: A = [a\u2081, ..., a_M], where each a_m \u2208 [0, 1] is the amplitude of the m-th basis function\n- Position parameters: P = [p\u2081, ..., p_{M+1}] \u2208 [-2,2]^{M+1}, defining the centers/positions of basis functions\n- Time points: T = [t\u2081, ..., t_M] \u2208 {-1, 0}^{M}, specifying whether each basis function is \"time-reversed\"\n\nRepresentation:\nh(x) = \u2211_{m=1}^{M} A[m] * ReLU(2*(x - P[m]) * (1 - |T[m]|)) \n\nThis piecewise-linear parameterization satisfies:\n1. h(x) \u2265 0\n2. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 by amplitude constraint\n3. Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = sum(A[i]*(1-p_reflected[i]))/2 = 1\n\nThe basis functions are localized triangular pulses of varying width but maximum height 1, ensuring h(x) \u2208 [0,1] and the sum of areas being exactly 1 (accounting for overlap).\n\nThe objective function is defined using the specified discrete grid:\n\nC\u2085 = dx * max_{k} [\u2211_{i} A[i] * \u2211_{j} (1 - A[j]) * I(|x_i - x_j - k| < (1 - |T|i||T[j]|)/2)]\n\nThe joint optimization of A, P, and T occurs in a constrained space with known bounds.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nPhase I: Basis construction validation\n  - Start from parent solution with N=350\n  - Decompose parent solution into M basis functions using top-ratioed singular value decomposition (with M determined adaptively)\n  - Verify parameter constraints and objective value\n\nPhase II: Interior-point optimization\n  - Formulate and solve a quasi-convex program in the A, P, T variables\n  - Use exponential normalizing functions to handle the amplitude constraints\n  - Implement projected gradient descent with dual averaging for global convergence\n  - Compute analytical subgradients for the max correlation term\n\nPhase III: Architecture extension\n  - Introduce new basis functions via local perturbation/FPO version\n  - Maintain a fixed dimensionality while extending the basis structure\n  - Use Bayesian active subspaces to maximize correlation reduction\n\nFinal checkpoint:\n  - Project any infeasible solutions by closest interpolation\n  - Verify both integral and bound constraints explicitly\n  - Re-evaluated all peaked architectures\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\nBase configuration:\n  - M = 8 basis functions\n  - Smoothness = 1.5 (controls pulse width)\n  - Basis configuration: Triangular pulse kernel with fixed height\n  - Genetic crossover rate = 0.7\n\nActive architectural variations:\n  I. Coverage density modulator (CDM):\n      \u03c1 \u2208 [0.5,0.8] (controls sparsity)\n      Affects data flow propagation and basis function distribution\n\n  II. Triangular degree booster (TDB):\n      d \u2208 {1,3,5} (polynomial degree of basis functions)\n      Changes basis function shape and responsiveness to correlations\n\n  III. Resonance weight expander (RWE):\n      w \u2208 [0.01,0.05] (weight assigned to low-correlation components)\n      Weight given in [0,1] in curvature optimization\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\nDominant costs:\n  - Optimization requires O(M\u00b2*N) per iteration (current M~8, N~350)\n  - Between 100-500 total optimization iterations allowed\n  - Check elapsed time after each restart or compromise precision accuracy\n\nImplementation:\n  - Phase I: 30% budget (330s) for fundamental validation\n  - Bayesian optimization: 40% budget (440s) with 10 independent trials\n  - Snr-based detection: 30% budget (330s) for catching hormesis effects\n\nFallback:\n  - Return parent solution with highest correlation found\n  - Also return evolved structure if constraints met\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\na. Preprocessing:\n  - Verify input arrays have complementary arrangement\n  - Using biased neural tangent kernel initialization\n\nb. Feasibility checks:\n  - 0 \u2264 h \u2264 1 enforced by sgd with barrier constraints\n  - Use of temporary storage for sensitivity analysis\n\nc. Objective calculation:\n  - Exactly matches parent's compute_c5 function\n  - All maximization steps accounted for\n\nd. Checkpoints:\n  - At least 7 preservation points with condition callbacks\n\nThe final candidate solution satisfies:\n  \u2211h[i]\u00b7dx = 1\n  \u2200i: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n\nWith mean/max correlation computed via the same method.",
